   <!---<LEFT>--->
   <TABLE CELLSPACING=20 CELLPADDING=0 WIDTH="540">
   <TR>

   <TD VALIGN=TOP COLSPAN=2>
   
	
<FONT SIZE=-1><FONT COLOR="#D60021">1.</FONT> <A HREF="../eda.htm"><FONT COLOR="#00105A">Exploratory Data Analysis</a></FONT></FONT><BR>
<FONT SIZE=-1><FONT COLOR="#D60021">1.3.</FONT> <A HREF="eda3.htm"><FONT COLOR="#00105A">EDA Techniques</a></FONT></FONT><BR>
<FONT SIZE=-1><FONT COLOR="#D60021">1.3.3.</FONT> <A HREF="eda33.htm"><FONT COLOR="#00105A">Graphical Techniques: Alphabetic</a></FONT></FONT><BR>
<BR>
<H2><FONT COLOR="#D60021">1.3.3.33.</FONT> 6-Plot </H2>
<FONT SIZE=-1>
</P>
</FONT>
   </TD>
   </TR>

   

   


<!-----begin jjf----->

<!-----begin section: purpose -->

<br>
<tr>
   <td width=15% valign=top>
      <I>Purpose:<br>
         Graphical model validation
      </I>
   </td>
   <td width=85% valign=top>
      The 6-plot is a collection of 6 specific graphical
      techniques whose purpose is to assess the validity
      of a Y versus X fit.  The fit can be a linear fit,
      a non-linear fit, a LOWESS (locally weighted least
      squares) fit, a spline fit, or any other fit utilizing
      a single independent variable.
      <p>
      The 6 plots are:
      <ol>
         <li>Response and predicted values versus the independent
             variable;
         <li>Residuals versus the independent variable;
         <li>Residuals versus the predicted values;
         <li>Lag plot of the residuals;
         <li>Histogram of the residuals;
         <li>Normal probability plot of the residuals.
      </ol>
   </td>
</tr>


<!-----begin section: Sample Plot -->

<br>
<tr>
   <td width=15% valign=top>
      <I>Sample Plot:<br>
      </I>
   </td>
   <td width=85% valign=top>

      <img src = "../gif/6plot.gif">
      <p>
      This 6-plot, which followed a linear fit, shows that the
      linear model is not adequate.  It suggests that a quadratic
      model would be a better model.
   </td>
</tr>


<!-----begin section: Importance -->

<br>
<tr>
   <td width=15% valign=top>
      <I>Importance:<br>
         Validating model
      </I>
   </td>
   <td width=85% valign=top>
      A model involving a response variable and a single independent
      variable has the form:
      <ul>
         y = f(x) + error
      </ul>
      where y is the response variable, x is the independent 
      variable, f is the linear or non-linear fit function, and
      error is the random component.  For a good model, the
      error component should behave like:
      <ol>
         <li>random drawings (i.e., independent);
         <li>from a fixed distribution;
         <li>with fixed location; and
         <li>with fixed variation.
      </ol>
      In addition, for fitting models it is usually furher assumed
      that the fixed distribution is normal and the fixed location
      is zero.  In addition, for a good model the fixed variation
      should be as small as possible.  A neccessary component
      of fitting models is to verify these assumptions for the
      error component and to assess whether the variation for
      error component is sufficently small.  The histogram,
      lag plot, and normal probability plot are used to
      verify the fixed distributuin, location, and variation
      assumptions on the error component.  The plot
      of the response variable and the predicted values versus
      the independent variable is used to assess whether the
      variation is sufficiently small.  The plots of the
      residuals versus the independent variable and the predicted
      values is used to assess the independence assumption.
      <p>
      Assessing the validity and quality of the fit in terms of
      the above assumptions is an absolutely vital part of
      the model fitting process.  No fit should be considered
      complete without an adequate model validation step.
   </td>
</tr>


<!-----begin section: questions -->
<tr>
   <td width=15% valign=top>
      <I>Questions</I>
   </td>
   <td width=85% valign=top>
      The 6-plot can be used to answer the following questions:
      <ol>
         <li>Are the residuals normally distributed with a fixed
             location and scale?
         <li>Are there outliers?
         <li>Is the fit adequate?
         <li>Do the residuals suggest a better fit?
      </ol>
   </td>
</tr>

<!-----begin section: input -->

<tr>
   <td width=15% valign=top>
      <I>Input:<br>
         Response variable Y, independent variable X
      </I>
   </td>
   <td width=85% valign=top>
      There is a response variable Y and an independent variable X.
      These variables should have the same number of oservations.
      That is, there are 2 columns of data with the same number of
      rows.
   </td>
</tr>

<!-----begin section: definition -->

<tr>
   <td width=15% valign=top>
      <I>Definition:<br>
         6 component plots
      </I>
   </td>
   <td width=85% valign=top>
       The 6-plot consists of the following:
       <ol>
          <li>Response and predicted values
             <ul>
                <li>Vertical axis: Response variable, predicted values
                <li>Horizontal axis: Independent variable
             </ul>
          <li>Residuals versus independent variable
             <ul>
                <li>Vertical axis: Residuals
                <li>Horizontal axis: Independent variable
             </ul>
          <li>Residuals versus predicted values
             <ul>
                <li>Vertical axis: Residuals
                <li>Horizontal axis: Predicted values
             </ul>
          <li>Lag plot of residuals
             <ul>
                <li>Vertical axis: RES(I)
                <li>Horizontal axis: RES(I-1)
             </ul>
          <li>Histogram of residuals
             <ul>
                <li>Vertical axis: Counts
                <li>Horizontal axis: Residual values
             </ul>
          <li>Normal probability plot of residuals
             <ul>
                <li>Vertical axis: Ordered residuals
                <li>Horizontal axis: Theoretical values from a
                    normal N(0,1) for ordered residuals
             </ul>
       </ol>
   </td>
</tr>

<!-----begin section: stat category -->

<tr>
   <td width=15% valign=top>
      <I>Stat Category:<br>
         Statistical modelling
      </I>
   </td>
   <td width=85% valign=top>
      The 6-plot should be used for any model of the
      form y = f(x) + error, regardless of the form of the
      function f.  The assumptions regarding the error component
      are the same across a broad class of models and the 6-plot
      provides insight into the adequacy of any model of this
      form.
   </td>
</tr>

<!-----begin section: usage -->

<tr>
   <td width=15% valign=top>
      <I>Usage:<br>
         Heavy
      </I>
   </td>
   <td width=85% valign=top>
      The 6-plot should be routine for model validation of any
      fit of the form y = f(x) + error.  Even if the 6-plot is
      not used explicitly, the component plots of the 6-plot
      should be generated.  The 6-plot provides a convenient
      format for looking at a group of essential plots for
      performing model validation.  The multi-plot format allows
      easier comparison than looking at 6 individual plots.
   </td>
</tr>

<!-----begin section: references -->

<tr>
   <td width=15% valign=top>
      <I>Reference</I>
   </td>
   <td width=85% valign=top>
      Filliben, "<I> ? </I>", ? .
   </td>
</tr>

<!-----begin section: dataplot command -->

<tr>
   <td width=15% valign=top>
      <I>Dataplot Command</I>
   </td>
   <td width=85% valign=top>
      FIT Y X<br>
      6-PLOT Y X
   </td>
</tr>

<!-----begin section: related techniques -->

<tr>
   <td width=15% valign=top>
      <I>Related Techniques</I>
   </td>
   <td width=85% valign=top>
      Fit<br>
      Scatter Plot<br>
      Run Sequence Plot<br>
      Lag Plot<br>
      Normal Probability Plot<br>
      Histogram<br>
   </dl>
   </td>
</tr>

<!-----begin section: case study -->
<tr>
   <td width=15% valign=top>
      <I>Case Study</I>
   </td>
   <td width=85% valign=top>
